{"name":"Diamond Cut","hex":"#e9e9f0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"diamond","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A glimmering glass-like mineral that is an allotrope of carbon in which each atom is surrounded by four others in the form of a tetrahedron.","A gemstone made from this mineral."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of, or containing diamond, a diamond or diamonds.","Of, relating to, or being a sixtieth anniversary."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/diamond"},{"term":"cut","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Teutila Cuicatec."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To incise, to cut into the surface of something.\nTo perform an incision on, for example with a knife.\n\nTo divide with a knife, scissors, or another sharp instrument.","To perform an incision on, for example with a knife."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cut"}],"entries":[{"title":"Diamond cut","blocks":[{"text":"A diamond cut is a style or design guide used when shaping a diamond for polishing such as the brilliant cut. Cut refers to shape, and also the symmetry, proportioning and polish of a diamond. The cut of a diamond greatly affects a diamond's brilliance—a poorly-cut diamond is less luminous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Diamond_cut_history.svg/330px-Diamond_cut_history.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_cut"},{"title":"Diamond Cut (Bonnie Tyler album)","blocks":[{"text":"Diamond Cut is the third studio album by Welsh singer Bonnie Tyler. It was released in February 1979 by RCA Records."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0b/Bonnie_Tyler_-_Diamond_Cut_album_artwork.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_Cut_(Bonnie_Tyler_album)"},{"title":"Diamond","blocks":[{"text":"Diamond is a mineral form of the element carbon with its atoms arranged in a crystal structure called diamond cubic. Diamond is a tasteless, odorless, strong, brittle solid, a poor conductor of electricity, colorless in pure form, and insoluble in water. Another solid form of carbon known as graphite is the chemically stable form of carbon at room temperature and pressure, but diamond is metastable and converts to it at a negligible rate under those conditions. Diamond has the highest hardness and thermal conductivity of any natural material, properties that are used in major industrial applications such as cutting and polishing tools."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Rough_Diamond.jpg/330px-Rough_Diamond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Brillanten.jpg/330px-Brillanten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Brillanten.jpg/330px-Brillanten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrillanten.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/British_Museum_The_Islamic_world_Necklace_Kundan_India_21022019_7703.jpg/500px-British_Museum_The_Islamic_world_Necklace_Kundan_India_21022019_7703.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/British_Museum_The_Islamic_world_Necklace_Kundan_India_21022019_7703.jpg/1280px-British_Museum_The_Islamic_world_Necklace_Kundan_India_21022019_7703.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/British_Museum_The_Islamic_world_